All working. Here's the final program:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata;

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web)
{
    TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver()
};

// 1) GetTypeInfo<T>() — strongly-typed metadata
JsonTypeInfo<Person> personInfo = options.GetTypeInfo<Person>();
string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new Person("Alice", 42), personInfo);

// 2) TryGetTypeInfo<T>() — probe without throwing
var empty = new JsonSerializerOptions { TypeInfoResolver = JsonTypeInfoResolver.Combine() };
if (empty.TryGetTypeInfo<Order>(out JsonTypeInfo<Order>? orderInfo))
    Console.WriteLine(orderInfo!.Type.Name);
else
    Console.WriteLine("metadata not available");
```

**Key APIs (new in .NET 11):**

| Method | Returns | Throws if missing? |
|--------|---------|-------------------|
| `options.GetTypeInfo<T>()` | `JsonTypeInfo<T>` | Yes |
| `options.TryGetTypeInfo<T>(out ...)` | `bool` | No |

The "empty resolver" trick (`JsonTypeInfoResolver.Combine()` with no args) gives you an options instance that resolves nothing — perfect for demonstrating the `false` path of `TryGetTypeInfo<T>`.
